    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d205">

                <pb facs="0303" n="187" sameAs="frus:frus1914/pg187"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 837.13/27.</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of State
                        to Chargé Scholle.</hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram.]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Department of State</hi>, <lb/>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1914-10-03">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">October 3, 1914</hi></date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Your October 1. Informally discuss with the President of Cuba the proposed
                    Amnesty Bill, and, should he seem inclined to approve the act, point out to him
                    the view of this Government that if the act should become law it would create an
                    unfortunate impression that crimes against person and reputation are allowed to
                    go without adequate punishment in Cuba. Make clear to the President that <pb
                        facs="0304" n="188" xml:id="pg188"/> the above suggestion arises from the
                    friendly interest taken by the Government of the United States in matters
                    affecting Cuba’s standing before the world.</p>
                <p>See previous instructions relative to the Amnesty Bill.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Lansing</hi></signed>.</closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
